WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
[Upbeat instrumental music]
One customer. Two charges. Your application asks the model which team should handle the ticket. The answer is billing.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
A small CPU fast path can return familiar answers. For short texts, embedding and classification take about fifty microseconds.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Different words can lead to the same decision. A learned classifier recognizes the recurring task, beyond exact text repeats.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Not every request qualifies. Pass those requests to decisio. The model stays available, and the application keeps its answer interface.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Teach from human-labelled requests, or collect model answers. These examples train a small candidate for one recurring decision.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Check separate examples and the saving on your host. Activate only a candidate that qualifies. Otherwise, keep the model path.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Register shared rules once as context. Send changing facts as state. The classifier reads facts; the model gets both.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Route recurring billing and access requests on the CPU. Keep unfamiliar support requests on the model path.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Moderation works the same way. Keep the policy fixed, classify each new post, and send ambiguous cases to the model.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.000
For refunds, keep the policy fixed. Send delivery age and item condition. Answer yes or no; pass exceptions on.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Register the task, shared context and fast table option. Ask with the same task identifier and new facts.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Let familiar decisions take the shorter route. Keep the model for everything else. Start with one task, and see what qualifies.
